Ok now i want us to implement the configuration screen. 

So the way it works right now we select a file on the lua graph configuration screen and it just shows us a preview of the main script. I want us to change this. If the main script returns a table that contains perhaps a configuration_ui key then we call that instead and we show this as the configuration screen. We should probably have a back operation that takes us back to the file/paste controls screen. 

this configuration_ui function exposes a gap in the current architecture, which is stateful UI. To facilitate this i propose we change the contract of the lua ui top level table such that it can contain 2 keys, one is the ui/layout instructions (as we already have), and the other is a state object. The state object is just a lua table. When we call the ui function the first time we would pass in an instance of this object that we had encoded in the database, if we were returning to/editing an existing graph, and nothing if it was a new graph instance. Graphs have configuration in the database, we need lua graphs to contain some nullable string encoded (probably json encoded version) of a lua table (of course when encoding this we only traverse basic data-types and ignore anything that's a function). 

We also need interactions from the UI to mutate state. To begin with i suggest we just add an on click modifier which is just a function. I think it should exist on basically all the ui types. From the kotlin side it's a lua function, and when you tap the element it calls that lua function and then calls some callback to invalidate the UI. The UI invalidation triggers the root level in compose to re-call the render function (this time without passing in the state), and it updates the full UI. I'm hoping we don't need to worry about race conditions here where the click callback is called on an old lua value, because both the new and old values would reference the same outer scope. Once the new render function is complete, it returns our new ui (which we render) and our new state (which we pass up to the view model to store). When the graph is created/updated we encode the last state object we saw in the database alongside the graph package. 

Finally when we come to render the graph now we need to pass in the data and context, but also the configuration. The app will decode that configuration from json and re-encode it as a luavalue when rendering the graph. 

One other thing comes to mind here which is that currently the lua graph package structure contains the keys data and render. I think these names are too generic and if we add more expansions to the root contract they might feel frustratingly non-descriptive later. We should think of better names for them.

